Notebook[{Cell["Time-Domain Terahertz Spectroscopy Tools", "Title"], 
  Cell["This small library is designed mainly for processing time-domain data \
in __transmission optics__. It includes:\n\n- signal (kernel) \
deconvolution;\n- seamless conversion between the time and frequency \
domains;\n- projections from linear to circular bases;\n- extraction of \
material parameters (absorption, refractive index, etc.), including \
__multiple-beam propagation and Fresnel losses__;\n- __advanced 1D phase \
unwrapping__ guided by the Kramers\[Dash]Kronig relation;\n- optical-response \
modeling for thin and thick slabs;\n- band-pass filtering and apodization of \
time traces;\n\nAll functions are strongly typed but also support multiple \
input patterns: time-domain data is automatically converted to the frequency \
domain if needed. Iterative solvers are implemented in OpenCL, with C \
functions running on the CPU as a fallback and a WL implementation serving as \
a secondary fallback if the C version fails to load.\n\n> Github repository \
https://github.com/JerryI/THzTDSTools\n\nLet's get started!", "Text"], 
  Cell["PacletDirectoryLoad[NotebookDirectory[]]; ", "Input"], 
  Cell["Get[\"JerryI`THzTDSTools`\"]\n({#1, Pane[Text[Information[#1, \
\"Usage\"]], 300]} & ) /@ Names[\"JerryI`THzTDSTools`*\"]; \nTableForm[%]", 
   "Input"], Cell["Material parameter extraction", "Section"], 
  Cell["Here is an example of a typical THz-TDS workflow using data measured \
with a *Toptica TeraFlash (2013)* spectrometer.", "Text"], 
  Cell["(* LOAD YOUR DATA *)\
\nShallow[sam]", "Input"], Cell["ListLinePlot[{sam, ref}, PlotLegends -> \
{\"Sample\", \"Reference\"}, AxesLabel -> {\"\[Tau] (ps)\", \"Signal\"}, \
PlotRange -> Full]", "Input"], Cell["The sample and reference \
(empty-aperture) signals must be assigned exact units (`\"Seconds\"` or \
`\"Millimeters\"` of delay-stage motion).", "Text"], 
  Cell[
   "{sam, ref} = (QuantityArray[#1, {\"Picoseconds\", 1}] & ) /@ {sam, ref}; \
", "Input"], Cell["Now we can plot both channels and adjust the range to the \
region with the highest SNR.", "Text"], 
  Cell["{fsam, fref} = FrequencySpectrumTrim /@ TD2FD[{sam, ref}]", "Input"], 
  Cell["`TD2FD` returns an array of triplets containing frequency, amplitude, \
and phase\[LongDash]that is, in Euler form.", "Text"], 
  Cell["Normal[First[fsam]]", "Input"], Cell["Let's plot it.", "Text"], 
  Cell["ToStandardOpticsUnits /@ {fsam, fref}; \n%[[All,All,{1, 2}]]; \
\nListLinePlot[%, Frame -> True, PlotRange -> Full, FrameLabel -> {\"freq. \
(THz)\", \"Amplitude\"}, PlotLegends -> {\"Sample\", \"Reference (hole)\"}]", 
   "Input"], Cell["In linear-response theory, the resulting sample signal is \
the convolution of the <abbr title=\"impulse response \
function\">IRF</abbr>(or transmission function) and the probe signal \
(reference):\n\n$$\n\\\\text{sample}(\\\\tau) = \
\\\\text{reference}(\\\\tau)~\\\\ast~t(\\\\tau)\n$$\n\nThe function \
$t(\\\\tau)$, or its Fourier transform $t(\\\\omega)$, contains all \
information about the sample.\n\n:::note\nWe recommend performing all \
transformations in the time domain whenever possible.\n:::\n\nLet's explore \
this:", "Text"], Cell["t = DeconvolveTraces[sam, ref]; \
\nListLinePlot[ToStandardOpticsUnits[t], Frame -> True, PlotRange -> Full, \
PlotRange -> Full, FrameLabel -> {\"\[Tau] (ps)\", \"t(\[Tau])\"}, PlotLabel \
-> \"Time-domain\"]\nToStandardOpticsUnits[FrequencySpectrumTrim[TD2FD[t]]]; \
\nListLinePlot[%[[All,{1, 2}]], Frame -> True, PlotRange -> Full, PlotRange \
-> Full, FrameLabel -> {\"\[Omega] (THz)\", \"|t(\[Omega])|\"}, PlotLabel -> \
\"Frequency-domain\"]", "Input"], Cell["The modulus of $t(\\\\omega)$ \
corresponds to the transmission spectrum. Given the thickness of the measured \
sample and the aperture difference between the sample and reference \
measurements, the **absorption coefficient** $\\\\alpha(\\\\omega)$ can be \
estimated:", "Text"], Cell["EstimateMaterialParameter[\"\[Alpha]\"][t, \
Quantity[0.6, \"Millimeters\"], \"Gain\" -> 1.2, Method -> \"Basic\"]; \
\nSelect[ToStandardOpticsUnits[%], #1[[1]] > 0.3 && #1[[1]] < 3.65 & ]; \
\nListLinePlot[%, Frame -> True, PlotRange -> Full, FrameLabel -> {\"\[Omega] \
(THz)\", \"\[Alpha] (1/cm)\"}]", "Input"], Cell["Advanced methods", 
   "Section"], Cell["The `\"Basic\"` method \
assumes:\n$$\n\\\\alpha(\\\\omega) = - \\\\frac{16 n_0^2}{(n_0+1)^4} \
\\\\frac{2}{d} ~log~|t(\\\\omega)|,\n$$\n\nwhere $n_0$ is the average \
refractive index, estimated automatically from the pulse delay.\nThis \
equation holds for *optically thick samples* with strong absorption. In the \
general case, however, $\\\\alpha$ is:\n\n$$\n\\\\alpha(\\\\omega) = \
\\\\frac{2 \\\\omega~\\\\Im~n(\\\\omega)}{c}\n$$\n\nTo estimate $n$ from the \
complex transmission function $t$, one must iteratively solve the Fresnel \
equations: <cite >[Withayachumnankul, W., Ferguson, B., Rainsford, T., \
Mickan, S. P., & Abbott, D. (2005). Material parameter extraction for \
terahertz time-domain spectroscopy using fixed-point iteration. Proceedings \
of SPIE, 5649, 142\[Dash]153. doi.org]</cite>\n\nThe most general material \
response for a slab-like sample is modeled as:\n\n$$\nt(\\\\omega)= \
\\\\underbrace{\\\\frac{4 n(\\\\omega)}{[1+ \
n(\\\\omega)]^2}}_{\\\\text{interface transmission}} \
\\\\underbrace{\\\\exp\\\\!\\\\left[i\\\\frac{\\\\omega L}{c}\\\\bigl( \
n(\\\\omega)-1\\\\bigr)\\\\right]}_{\\\\text{single pass}} \
\\\\underbrace{\\\\frac{1}{ 1-\\\\left(\\\\frac{ n(\\\\omega)-1}{ \
n(\\\\omega)+1}\\\\right)^2 \\\\exp\\\\!\\\\left(2i\\\\frac{\\\\omega L}{c} \
n(\\\\omega)\\\\right) }}_{\\\\text{Fabry\[Dash]P\[EAcute]rot \
term}}\n$$\n\n\n### Phase unwrapping\n`TD2FD` stores the principal Fourier \
phase in the range from $-\\\\pi$ to $\\\\pi$. A sampled wrapped phase does \
not, by itself, tell us how many full $2\\\\pi$ turns occur between frequency \
bins. The usual nearest-neighbor unwrap therefore becomes unreliable when the \
phase changes rapidly or the transmission reaches a deep minimum (for \
example, near a sharp excitation). This library offers a Kramers\[Dash]Kronig \
(KK)-guided method that uses the measured magnitude to help choose those \
turns.\n\nHere are all the built-in methods for comparison:", "Text"], 
  Cell["$methods = {None, \"Basic\", \"KK\", \"KKGuided\", \
\"KKGuidedMultiscale\"}; \n(UnwrapPhase[t, Method -> #1, \
\"RemoveLinearPhase\" -> True] & ) /@ $methods; \nToStandardOpticsUnits /@ \
FrequencySpectrumTrim /@ %; \nListLinePlot[%[[All,All,{1, 3}]], Frame -> \
True, PlotRange -> {{0.25, 3.}, {-5, 20}}, FrameLabel -> {\"\[Omega] (THz)\", \
\"\[Phi]\"}, PlotLegends -> ToString /@ $methods, PlotLabel -> \"Phase \
unwrapping\"]", "Input"], Cell["The difference between `KKGuided` and \
`KKGuidedMultiscale` is that the latter also considers neighboring bins to \
verify that the local background level remains consistent. ==The latter \
method outperforms the others in this particular case==.", "Text"], 
  Cell["### Iterative method\nBy setting `Method` to `\"Iterative\"`, we can \
estimate the material parameters more accurately by using almost all of the \
information available in the measured time traces. Let's compare the two \
methods.", "Text"], Cell["unwrapped = UnwrapPhase[t, Method -> \
\"KKGuidedMultiscale\"]; \
\nListLinePlot[{EstimateMaterialParameter[\"\[Alpha]\", unwrapped, \
Quantity[0.6, \"Millimeters\"], \"Gain\" -> 1.2], \
EstimateMaterialParameter[\"\[Alpha]\", unwrapped, Quantity[0.6, \
\"Millimeters\"], Method -> \"Iterative\", \"Gain\" -> 1.2]}, PlotRange -> \
{{0.2, 4.}, {-20, 160}}, Frame -> True, FrameLabel -> {\"f (THz)\", \
\"\[Alpha] (1/cm)\"}, PlotLegends -> {\"-LogT / d\", \"Iterative solver for n \
and k\"}]", "Input"], Cell["==Note that, in this case, we neglected the \
Fabry\[Dash]P\[EAcute]rot term.==\n\nFor this relatively thick sample, we \
cannot observe a significant difference. However, the difference may become \
critical for submillimeter samples. Let's compare the real parts of the \
refractive indices as well.", "Text"], Cell["ListLinePlot[{EstimateMaterialPa\
rameter[\"n\", unwrapped, Quantity[0.6, \"Millimeters\"], \"Gain\" -> 1.2], \
EstimateMaterialParameter[\"n\", unwrapped, Quantity[0.6, \"Millimeters\"], \
Method -> \"Iterative\", \"Gain\" -> 1.2]}, PlotRange -> {{0.2, 4.}, {3.8, \
5.2}}, Frame -> True, FrameLabel -> {\"f (THz)\", \"\[Alpha] (1/cm)\"}, \
PlotLegends -> {\"-LogT / d\", \"Iterative solver for n and k\"}]", "Input"], 
  Cell["### Fabry\[Dash]P\[EAcute]rot deconvolution\nFinally, to take full \
advantage of the time-domain method, we should deconvolve the material \
parameters from the internal reflections that produce oscillatory patterns in \
all spectra. To enable deconvolution, simply set:", "Text"], 
  Cell["Method -> {\"Iterative\", \"SolveFP\" -> True}", "Input"], 
  Cell["However, this usually does not work on the first attempt. ==FP \
deconvolution is extremely sensitive to slight inaccuracies in thickness \
measurements or differences in aperture between the sample and reference==. \
Therefore, we need to vary `\"Gain\"` and thickness slightly, as well as the \
static phase shift `\"PhaseCorrection\"`. Note that `\"PhaseCorrection\"` can \
only be $\\\\pm Z ~ 2\\\\pi$; it originates from noisy low-frequency data, \
which can produce such phase jumps. This is essentially a multivariable \
optimization problem.\n\nTo find the best (or true) combination, we use an \
estimated value generated by `EstimateMaterialParameter[\"FP Reduction\"]`, \
which is essentially the relative amplitude of the FP oscillations compared \
with the background. Here, we find it using a grid search:", "Text"], 
  Cell["Module[{band = Interval[{Quantity[1.5, \"Terahertz\"], Quantity[2.5, \
\"Terahertz\"]}], temporal = {}}, Monitor[Table[temporal = Append[temporal, \
{d, gain, EstimateMaterialParameter[\"FP Reduction\", unwrapped, Quantity[d, \
\"Millimeters\"], \"Gain\" -> gain, \"FPInterval\" -> band]}]; \
temporal[[-1]], {d, 0.6, 0.7, 0.0025}, {gain, 0.5, 1.6, 0.05}], \
ListDensityPlot[temporal, PlotRange -> {{0.6, 0.7}, {0.5, 1.6}}]]]; \
\nListContourPlot[data = Flatten[%, 1], FrameLabel -> {\"Thickness (mm)\", \
\"Gain\"}, PlotRange -> Full]\n  \nMaximalBy[data, Last]", "Input"], 
  Cell["The global minimum is `{0.637, 1.2}`. Let's subtract the FP \
contribution and compare it with the previous results:", "Text"], 
  Cell["ListLinePlot[{EstimateMaterialParameter[\"\[Alpha]\", unwrapped, \
Quantity[0.635, \"Millimeters\"], Method -> \"Iterative\", \"Gain\" -> 1.2], \
EstimateMaterialParameter[\"\[Alpha]\", unwrapped, Quantity[0.635, \
\"Millimeters\"], Method -> {\"Iterative\", \"SolveFP\" -> True}, \"Gain\" -> \
1.15]}, PlotRange -> {{0.2, 4.}, {-20, 160}}, PlotStyle -> \
{ColorData[116][2], ColorData[116][3]}, Frame -> True, FrameLabel -> \
{\"\[Omega] (THz)\", \"\[Alpha] (1/cm)\"}, PlotLegends -> {\"Iterative solver \
for n and k\", \"Iterative solver for n and k + FP reduction\"}]", "Input"], 
  Cell["We can also recover the time trace using the dedicated function \
`DeconvolveFP`:", "Text"], Cell["FD2TD[DeconvolveFP[unwrapped, \
Quantity[0.635, \"Millimeters\"], \"Gain\" -> 1.2]]; \nToStandardOpticsUnits \
/@ {t, %}; \nListLinePlot[%, PlotRange -> Full, Frame -> True, FrameLabel -> \
{\"\[Tau] (ps)\", \"t(\[Tau])\"}, PlotLegends -> {\"Original\", \"FP \
removed\"}]", "Input"], Cell["In addition, we can filter the range of \
interest using a **band-pass filter**.", "Text"], 
  Cell["FD2TD[DeconvolveFP[unwrapped, Quantity[0.635, \"Millimeters\"], \
\"Gain\" -> 1.1]]; \n(BandPassFilter[#1, Quantity[1.25, \"Terahertz\"], 2] & \
) /@ {t, %}; \nToStandardOpticsUnits /@ %; \nListLinePlot[%, PlotRange -> \
Full, Frame -> True, FrameLabel -> {\"\[Tau] (ps)\", \"t(\[Tau])\"}, \
PlotLegends -> {\"Original\", \"FP removed\"}]", "Input"], 
  Cell["This may provide additional information for further correcting the \
gain and thickness.", "Text"], Cell["Circular basis (MCD, NCD, and others)", 
   "Section"], Cell["Our library can transform data acquired with linearly \
polarized light, using a special arrangement of polarizer and analyzer \
angles, to retrieve the circular components of the light. This is helpful \
when the eigenstates of light inside the probed medium are circularly \
polarized (as in Faraday rotation, MCD, and other effects). In this example, \
the data were measured at a fixed polarizer angle in two subsequent \
measurements with $\\\\text{Analyzer} = \\\\text{Polarizer}\\\\pm \
45^\\\\circ$.\n\nAll transformations are performed in the time domain. Here \
is an example:", "Text"], Cell["(* LOAD YOUR DATA *)\n(* here we use example \
data stored in the notebook *)\n{sam, ref} = ... \nsam = \
(QuantityArray[#1, {\"Picoseconds\", 1}] & ) /@ sam; \nref = \
(QuantityArray[#1, {\"Picoseconds\", 1}] & ) /@ ref; ", "Input"], 
  Cell["This effectively switches the basis from linear to circular:", 
   "Text"], Cell["sam = LinearProjectionsToCircular[Sequence @@ sam, \
{-45*Degree, 45*Degree}]; \nref = LinearProjectionsToCircular[Sequence @@ \
ref, {-45*Degree, 45*Degree}]; ", "Input"], Cell["`sam` and `ref` **can now \
be treated as usual**, including for material-parameter extraction and \
Fabry\[Dash]P\[EAcute]rot deconvolution, **provided that the original data \
were measured using circularly polarized light**.", "Text"], 
  Cell["t = MapThread[DeconvolveTraces[#1, #2] & , {sam, ref}]; ", "Input"], 
  Cell["(EstimateMaterialParameter[\"\[Alpha]\", FrequencySpectrumTrim[#1], \
Quantity[0.08, \"Millimeters\"]] & ) /@ t; \nListLinePlot[%, PlotRange -> \
{{0.5, 3.}, {-150, 300}}, Frame -> True, PlotStyle -> {Red, Blue}, \
PlotLegends -> {\"CW\", \"CCW\"}]", "Input"], Cell["The signal is strongly \
affected by FP fringes. However, we can attempt to remove them and compare \
the results.", "Text"], Cell["unwrapped = (UnwrapPhase[#1, Method -> \
\"KKGuidedMultiscale\"] & ) /@ t; \nDeploy[With[{estimate = Function[fp, \
ListLinePlot[MapThread[Select[ToStandardOpticsUnits[EstimateMaterialParameter\
[\"\[Alpha]\", #1, Quantity[0.08, \"Millimeters\"], Method -> {\"Iterative\", \
\"SolveFP\" -> fp}, \"PhaseCorrection\" -> #2, \"Gain\" -> 0.6]], \
Function[xy, xy[[1]] >= 0.5 && xy[[1]] <= 3.2]] & , {unwrapped, {-4*Pi, 0}}], \
FrameLabel -> {\"\[Omega] (THz)\", \"\[Alpha] (1/cm)\"}, PlotRange -> {{0.5, \
3.}, Automatic}, Frame -> True, PlotStyle -> {Red, Blue}, PlotLegends -> \
{\"CW\", \"CCW\"}]]}, Row[Reverse[{Labeled[estimate[True], \"After\", Top], \
Labeled[estimate[False], \"Before\", Top]}]]]]", "Input"]}]
